|~ What is declared under `::doc`: the refusal of its constructor and
   the verb that reads a doc's text. ~|

:doc/construct
  |~~ The doc of a text. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:doc/PayloadNotStringError
  |~~ `::doc` builds a doc from its text, a string; a payload of another
      kind is refused.
  ~~|

:doc/PayloadNotStringError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::doc["a" "b"] !| [type /actualType] | eq [::doc/PayloadNotStringError ::vec])

:doc/content
  |~~ The text of the doc, prose whatever it writes: a `~(…)` in it is
      text, and code is named between backquotes.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:doc/content/keepsCodeAsText
  |~~ A quote written in a doc is text of it. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ a ~(add 1) b ~~| | content | eq " a ~(add 1) b ")

:doc/content/readsThePageOfAWord
  |~~ The content of a word's page is its text. ~~|
  ::law~(::vec/join | doc | content | contains "separator")

:doc/content/isThePayload
  |~~ The content of a doc is its payload. ~~|
  ::law~(|~~ prose ~~| | [content payload] | eq [" prose " " prose "])

:doc/content/text
  |~~ The text of the doc. ~~|
  ::string
